**Business Document: Enhancing Educational Services at Global Learning Institute**

**Overview:**
Global Learning Institute (GLI), located in San Francisco, California, is dedicated to improving educational standards and student services through comprehensive curriculum development and accreditation processes. 

**Key Entities:**
- **Organizations:**
  - Global Learning Institute (GLI)
  - Accreditation Board for Educator Preparation (ABEP)
  - Western Association of Schools and Colleges (WASC)
  - National Council for Accreditation of Teacher Education (NCATE)
  - California Department of Education (CDE)
  - American Association of Colleges for Teacher Education (AACTE)

- **Curriculum Development:**
  - STEM Curriculum Initiative led by Dr. Emily Rogers
  - Arts Integration Program managed by Prof. James Liu
  - Health Sciences Curriculum Review Committee

- **Student Services:**
  - Counseling Services led by Ms. Linda Patel
  - Career Development Office (CDO) directed by Mr. Robert Chen
  - Tutoring and Academic Support Services (TASS) facilitated by Ms. Sarah Green
  - Student Health Services (SHS) coordinated by Dr. Michael Adams

- **Key Programs:**
  - Early College High School (ECHS) collaboration with local high schools
  - Online Learning Platform (OLP) powered by Moodle
  - Study Abroad Program in partnership with universities in Europe
  - Internship Program with Silicon Valley tech companies

**Accreditation Process:**
- GLI is pursuing accreditation renewal from WASC by June 2024.
- Key steps include:
  - Self-Study Report preparation led by Dr. Aaron Smith
  - Stakeholder Feedback Sessions scheduled for March 2024
  - On-Site Review by WASC team on July 15-17, 2024
  
**Partnerships:**
- Collaborations with:
  - Stanford University for joint research initiatives
  - University of California, Berkeley for curriculum alignment
  - Local NGOs for community service projects
- Dependency on support from the California State Legislature for funding educational programs.

**Goals for 2024:**
- Achieve a 15% increase in student enrollment in STEM fields.
- Enhance student satisfaction ratings to above 85% in annual surveys.
- Expand mental health services by hiring 3 additional counselors by September 2024.

